  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nA non-VAT-registered seller cannot issue a VAT invoice. Under Section 236 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), only VAT-registered persons are authorized to issue VAT invoices. This regulation stipulates that a person must register for VAT if their gross sales exceed the threshold set by law, and only those registered can issue invoices that reflect VAT.\n\nAdditionally, Revenue Regulation No. 16-2005 clarifies that a \"VAT-registered person\" is one who has been registered as a VAT taxpayer under Section 236 of the NIRC. Therefore, if a seller is not registered for VAT, they are not permitted to issue VAT invoices, as doing so would misrepresent their tax status and obligations. \n\nIn summary, the legal basis for this conclusion is found in:\n- **NIRC Section 236**: Registration requirements for VAT.\n- **RR 16-2005**: Definitions and clarifications regarding VAT registration and invoicing.\n\n### Relevant Facts / Assumptions\nThe factual circumstances have not been fully described. The analysis is based on the question as presented.\n\n### Alternative Interpretations\nNo alternative interpretations identified from the available indexed authorities.\n\n### Position Strength\nPosition strength is preliminary and subject to full fact pattern verification.",
